From e3b6a9222c13ae5a46a9bcadd781064ce7124156 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 21:40:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH v2 12/13] hv: netvsc: allocate RNDIS request descriptors with
 kvzalloc_obj()

struct rndis_request embeds two RNDIS_EXT_LEN protocol tails.
RNDIS_EXT_LEN is HV_HYP_PAGE_SIZE, so the two tails alone are 8 KiB and
the struct is just over KMALLOC_MAX_CACHE_SIZE. get_rndis_request()
therefore falls through kzalloc_obj() to kmalloc_large(), which asks for
an order-2 compound page on every RNDIS control request.

That is a high-order allocation on the subchannel open path. The
fragmentation drill of this series reproduces it: with the buddy holding
a single order-2 block, the hv_netvsc rebind reaches

  kworker/0:1 invoked oom-killer:
    gfp_mask=0x40dc0(GFP_KERNEL|__GFP_ZERO|__GFP_COMP), order=2
  Workqueue: events netvsc_subchan_work
  ? ___kmalloc_large_node
  ? __kmalloc_large_noprof
  ? get_rndis_request
  ? rndis_set_rss_param_msg
  ? rndis_set_subchannel
  ? netvsc_subchan_work
  sh: page allocation failure: order:2, ...
  Kernel panic - not syncing: System is deadlocked on memory

Patch 11/13 removes the order-7 requestor array from that path. This
removes the order-2 request descriptor, which is the last high-order
allocation on the subchannel open path.

The two tails are protocol capacity, not padding: an RNDIS response is
memcpy'd into &request->response_msg and may spill contiguously into
response_ext, guarded by

  resp->msg_len <= sizeof(struct rndis_message) + RNDIS_EXT_LEN

so shrinking them would be a behaviour change and is not what this
patch does.

The request is a control-path descriptor copied into the VMBus ring and
is never DMA-mapped from this allocation, so vmalloc backing carries no
Confidential Computing implication -- the same reasoning as the
kvcalloc() requestor array in 11/13. kvzalloc_obj() is the type-safe
counterpart of kzalloc_obj() that keeps the zeroing, and it tries the
kmalloc path with __GFP_NORETRY|__GFP_NOWARN first, so it neither fails
hard nor reaches the OOM killer when no compound page is available. The
zeroing is load bearing and is not dropped: response_ext and request_ext
are read by the protocol after a response arrives, and an uninitialised
tail would be an information leak.

Free side is kfree(req) in put_rndis_request(); it must become kvfree()
because the allocation may now come from vmalloc.

Revert this patch if rndis_set_subchannel() or netvsc_subchan_work()
reports -ENOMEM from get_rndis_request() again, if a
page-allocation-failure call trace reaches get_rndis_request(), or if
any path begins to treat the request descriptor as host-visible or
direct-mapped memory.

Signed-off-by: Emerson Busson <emersonbusson@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/net/hyperv/rndis_filter.c | 19 +++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 17 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/hyperv/rndis_filter.c b/drivers/net/hyperv/rndis_filter.c
index 9b6c44979b4e..2c70c59cdc0e 100644
--- a/drivers/net/hyperv/rndis_filter.c
+++ b/drivers/net/hyperv/rndis_filter.c
@@ -87,7 +87,21 @@ static struct rndis_request *get_rndis_request(struct rndis_device *dev,
 	struct rndis_set_request *set;
 	unsigned long flags;
 
-	request = kzalloc_obj(struct rndis_request);
+	/*
+	 * struct rndis_request embeds two RNDIS_EXT_LEN (4 KiB) protocol
+	 * tails and is larger than KMALLOC_MAX_CACHE_SIZE, so
+	 * kzalloc_obj() hands back an order-2 compound page. That is a
+	 * high-order allocation on the subchannel open path and it fails
+	 * under buddy fragmentation -- the same failure this series
+	 * removes from the rings and the requestor array. The request is
+	 * a control-path object copied into the VMBus ring and is never
+	 * DMA-mapped from this allocation, so vmalloc backing is fine.
+	 * kvzalloc_obj() keeps the zeroing kzalloc_obj() provided and
+	 * tries the kmalloc path with __GFP_NORETRY|__GFP_NOWARN first,
+	 * so it neither fails hard nor reaches the OOM killer when no
+	 * compound page is available.
+	 */
+	request = kvzalloc_obj(struct rndis_request);
 	if (!request)
 		return NULL;
 
@@ -124,7 +138,8 @@ static void put_rndis_request(struct rndis_device *dev,
 	list_del(&req->list_ent);
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&dev->request_lock, flags);
 
-	kfree(req);
+	/* Paired with the kvzalloc_obj() in get_rndis_request(). */
+	kvfree(req);
 }
 
 static void dump_rndis_message(struct net_device *netdev,
